          %p Very incomplete!
          %p (I wasn’t sure how to title this page, so the name just mentions projective Banach(able) spaces, even though I hope to eventually get onto injective Banach(able) spaces.)
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a strictly positive constant. We say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is
            %strong C-projective
            or
            %strong
              projective with constant
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            if, whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a Banach space,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a closed subspace of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:P\to Y/X
            is a continuous linear map, there exists a continuous linear map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:P\to X
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vert g \Vert \leq C\Vert f\Vert
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi qg
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} qg=f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:Y\to Y/X
            is the canonical quotient map.
          %p
            Semadeni (see below for reference) gives a slightly different definition. Slightly paraphrased, it reads as follows:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a
            = succeed "-projective" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ban
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ban_1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+linear+map"} short linear map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: F\to H
            and any short linear map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi: G\to H
            which satisfies
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ball
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Ball
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(Ball(G))=Ball(H)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi:F\to G
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi=\psi\pi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ball
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ball(\cdot)
            denotes the closed unit ball of a given Banach space.
          %p
            The condition that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Ball
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Ball
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(Ball(G))=Ball(H)
            is stronger than requiring the canonical induced map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ker
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/\ker\phi \to H
            to be an isometric isomorphism. For instance, let us work momentarily with real Banach spaces, and consider the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi:c_0 \to \mathbb{R}
            defined by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi((x_n)) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty x_n/2^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is normed by saying that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            has norm
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ker
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_0/\ker(\phi)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is an isometry. But a little thought shows that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ∉
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ball
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\notin \phi(Ball(c_0))
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            be any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^1(S)
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+functor"} free
            Banach space on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bang
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bang
            denote the category of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ball
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ball
            be the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bang
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bang\to Set
            which sends a Banach space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to its closed unit ball
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ball
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ball(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^1
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ball
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ball
          %p
            In particular, for any set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and Banach space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bang
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Ball
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bang(\ell^1(S), V) \cong Set(S, Ball(V))
          %p
            (This is the more precise version of the statement “bounded linear maps from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^1(S)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            correspond to bounded
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p …
          %p
            Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^1(S)
            is a
            = succeed "-projective" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\gt1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is uncountable this definitely needs
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Choice"} Choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is countably infinite this can be relaxed to
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
          %blockquote
            %p
              David Roberts: Or even
              = succeed "?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
          %p …
          %p
            It turns out that every projective Banach(able) space is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
            as a Banach(able) space to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^1(S)
            for some set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            YC has often wondered if this result has any meaningful kinship with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nielsen-Schreier+theorem"} Nielsen-Schreier theorem
          %h2#comments_on_attempts_at_equivalent_formulations Comments on attempts at equivalent formulations
          %p
            In an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            one sometimes (often?) sees the following definition of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is projective in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(P,\cdot)
            is (right)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} exact
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphisms"} epimorphisms
          %blockquote
            %p Urs: yes, because the hom-functor preserves all limits.
          %p
            Now the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            of Banach spaces and Banachable spaces each possess notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+object"} zero object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
            of Banach(able) spaces does not imply exactness of the underlying short exact sequence of
            = succeed "!" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
          %p
            Put another way: as we (will) have defined it above, projectivity of a Banach space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is different from asking that the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{hom}(P,\cdot)
            preserves epimorphisms…
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Roberts"}> David Roberts
              \: I suppose it’s about asking what you want to do with said projective objects - projective resolutions? Calculate some sort of cohomology? Know that every epimorphism (or analogous map - perhaps the quotient map of a ses?) to a projective object splits?
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yemon+Choi"}> Yemon Choi
              \: Good question, David. The answer is “sort of” - my original background is in Hochschild cohomology of Banach algebras, so I am more concerned about Banach modules (over a fixed Banach algebra) that are projective/injective/flat
              %em in some appropriate sense
              … Now if one looks up Hochschild coho in something like Weibel’s book, defined for R-bimodules where R is a k-algebra, then the theory works much better if all short exact sequences in k-bimod split. This is fine, with Choice, if k is a field (i.e. we are working with vector spaces and usual algebras) but does not hold in general; and really what makes things work is that every object of Vect is projective in the presence of Choice, i.e. you always have a linear section.
            %p
              Now to get back to your question: it has been found from experience that the Banach version of Hochschild coho is more likely to behave like the classical case when your algebra and module both have underlying Banach space “toplinearly isomorphic” to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℓ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^1
              is OK in some circumstances. What seems to be going on here is that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℓ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^1
              is a projective Banach space and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^1
              a flat Banach space… hence my interest, hence this page.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#projective_in_which_category Projective in which category?
          %h3#kthes_theorem Köthe’s theorem?
          %h2#terminology_metrically_projective_and_metrically_injective Terminology: “metrically projective” and “metrically injective”
          %h2#injective_banach_spaces Injective Banach spaces
          %p Theorem of Kelley-Nachbin and others?
